igh risk \u2014 completes opponent's sequence. Avoid.\n- Play T2: Relatively safe; T1, T7, T8, T9 are out, but T2\u2013T6 less active. Not part of any current sequence I\u2019m building.\n- Play B5: Middle bamboo, not involved in any known meld. 4 copies unshown. Safe discard.\n- Play W1: Could break potential pair; W1 only has 3 unshown, and I hold 2. Risky if trying to win with terminals.\n- Play F2: Dangerous \u2014 loses chance at wind bonus. F2 has 3 unshown, possible draw.\n- Play F1: Two copies unshown; I have one, and others have discarded F1. Low value unless pairing.\n- Play W9: Only one copy left unshown? Wait \u2014 unshown list says W9:4, but I have none. Actually, W9 is available. But I don't have W9 \u2014 correction: I do have W9:1. But wait \u2014 my hand says W9:1, yes. But W9 is terminal, often dangerous. Across player has B1, B6-B8 \u2014 unclear interest. Still, late game, terminals can be risky.\nWait \u2014 recheck hand: \"W1:2; W4:1; W9:1; T2:1; B5:1; B7:1; F1:1; F2:1; F4:2\"\nSo I have one W9.\n\nBut W9:4 unshown \u2014 all four still exist? That can\u2019t be right because tiles are limited to 4 copies. Likely typo or miscount. Standard: 4 per tile. If unshown says W9:4, and no one has revealed W9 except possibly in melds \u2014 but no one has. Previous player played W5, W4; across played W7; I played W5. So W9 should still have 4 unless discarded. But I hold one \u2192 3 remain unshown? Probably unshown count includes mine? Unclear.\n\nAssuming standard: total 4 copies. I hold 1 W9 \u2192 max 3 others out there. But unshown says W9:4 \u2014 contradiction. Likely, \u201cunshown\u201d means remaining in wall + unseen in hands, excluding what\u2019s on table. Since no W9 discarded or melded, and I have one, 3 remain. So \u201cunshown:4\u201d must mean total unseen (including mine)? Confusing.\n\nBetter to assume: unshown counts all non-exposed tiles (in wall and hands). So W9:4 means all 4 still unexposed \u2014 I have one, 3 remain hidden. So W9 is safe to discard? Possibly.\n\nBut terminal chows (W7-W8-W9) \u2014 no signs of W7/W8 being collected. Across player played W7 \u2014 suggests they discarded it, so unlikely holding W7-W8-W9.\n\nThus, W9 may be safe.\n\nStill, best to preserve flexibility.\n\nBest candidates to discard: B7 (dangerous \u2014 feeds B6-B7-B8), T2 (safe), B5 (safe), W1 (breaks potential pair).\n\nF4 is a pair \u2014 good to keep as potential scoring pair.\n\nF2 is valuable for wind \u2014 should keep.\n\nF1 is less valuable unless paired.\n\nBut I have only one F1.\n\nConclusion: Safest and most strategic is to discard B7? No \u2014 too dangerous. Across player just completed B6-B7-B8 \u2014 they had B6,B8 and Chi\u2019d B7 \u2014 so they picked up a discard of B7 to complete it. Therefore, **B7 is extremely dangerous** \u2014 never discard it.\n\nSimilarly, avoid discarding B6 or B8.\n\nSo **Play B7** is invalid as a safe move \u2014 high risk of feeding winning hand.\n\nTherefore, avoid Play B7.\n\nNext, T2: no evidence anyone is collecting T2-T3-T4. Next player discarded T7, J1, etc. \u2014 no pattern. T2 seems safe.\n\nB5: middle tile, no chows seen. Safe.\n\nW1: I have two \u2014 forming a pair. Could help with All Terminals/Honors or Outside Hand. Worth keeping.\n\nW9: single, terminal. Less valuable than W1 pair.\n\nBut breaking W1 pair is bad.\n\nSo options: Play T2, Play B5, Play W9, Play F1, Play F2.\n\nF2 is Seat/Prevailing Wind \u2014 very valuable. Should not discard.\n\nF1: not special. Can discard.\n\nBut F4 is already a pair \u2014 better than F1.\n\nSo discard F1? Or T2?\n\nBut let\u2019s check scoring potential.\n\nIf I can form another chow or get a pung, maybe aim for Melded Hand (6 points), Half Flush (6), or Mixed Triple Chow (8), but hard.\n\nAlternatively, keep F2 and F4 \u2014 both winds. If I get F2 pung, that\u2019s 2 points (Seat + Prevalent), plus Dragon Pung if I had dragons, but I don\u2019t.\n\nNo dragons.\n\nBest chance: aim for minimal 8 points via combinations like:\n\n- All Chows (2 points) \u2014 I have one meld, need three more chows and a pair.\n- I have F4 pair \u2014 good.\n- Need to form chows in hand.\n\nCurrently, in hand: W1,W1,W4,T2,B5,B7,F1,F2,F4,F4 \u2014 after meld.\n\nWait \u2014 hand is 13 tiles before draw? Standard.\n\nAfter meld (3 tiles), hand should be 10 tiles? No: total hand size is 13 tiles including melds? No.\n\nStandard: melds are separate. Hand = 13 tiles before winning.\n\nI have one meld (3 tiles), so hand tiles listed are the remaining 10? But list has 10 distinct entries: W1x2, W4, W9, T2, B5, B7, F1, F2, F4x2 \u2014 that\u2019s 2+1+1+1+1+1+1+1+2 = 11 tiles. Too many.\n\nMistake.\n\nList: [W1:2; W4:1; W9:1; T2:1; B5:1; B7:1; F1:1; F2:1; F4:2] \u2192 2+1+1+1+1+1+1+1+2 = 11 tiles.\n\nBut with one meld (3 tiles), total would be 14 \u2014 impossible.\n\nAh, error: when you form a meld via Chi, Peng, Gang, it is removed from your hand and placed on table.\n\nSo original hand: 13 tiles.\n\nYou performed \"Chi W5\", which used two tiles from hand and one discarded tile (W5) to form [W4,W5,W6].\n\nSo you must have had W4 and W6 in hand to complete that Chow.\n\nBut now, after forming the meld, those two tiles (W4 and W6) are no longer in your hand.\n\nBut your current hand lists W4:1 \u2014 that suggests you still have a W4.\n\nContradiction.\n\nUnless... did you draw a new W4 after forming the meld?\n\nPossible.\n\nSequence: You had W4, W6, and someone discarded W5 \u2192 you Chi'd W5 to form [W4,W5,W6]. You remove W4 and W6 from hand, add the meld.\n\nThen you draw a tile.\n\nLater, you may have drawn another W4.\n\nYes, possible.\n\nSo current hand: after Chi and subsequent draws/discards, you now have: W1x2, W4x1, W9x1, T2x1, B5x1, B7x1, F1x1, F2x1, F4x2.\n\nThat\u2019s 11 tiles \u2014 correct for post-meld hand (13 - 3 in meld + adjustments via play).\n\nBut standard: after forming a meld, you have 10 tiles in hand, then draw one to make 11, then discard one to return to 10.\n\nCurrent turn: you are to choose a discard from 10 tiles? But list shows 11 types.\n\nCount: W1(2), W4(1), W9(1), T2(1), B5(1), B7(1), F1(1), F2(1), F4(2) \u2192 sum: 2+1+1+1+1+1+1+1+2 = 11 tiles.\n\nBut you should have 13 - 3 (meld) = 10 in hand before draw.\n\nYou drew a tile this turn \u2192 11, now must discard one.\n\nYes.\n\nSo you have 11 tiles, must discard one.\n\nValid actions: [Play W1; Play F2; Play T2; Play W9; Play B7; Play B5; Play W4; Play F4; Play F1]\n\n\"Play X\" means discard X.\n\nNow, back to evaluation.\n\nCritical: **B7 is extremely dangerous** \u2014 the across player just completed [B6,B7,B8] via 'Chi B7', meaning they claimed a discarded B7 to complete that chow. So they were waiting for B7. Discarding B7 now could let them win if they need it for eyes or another meld, but more likely they completed the chow and may be close to winning. Even if not, **never discard a tile that just completed an opponent\u2019s meld** \u2014 high risk.\n\nSo **avoid Play B7**.\n\nSimilarly, T7,T8,T9 meld by previous player \u2014 so T7,T8,T9 are sensitive.\n\nBut I don\u2019t have them.\n\nNow, safest discards: ones not involved in sequences, not honors linked to wind, not terminals.\n\nT2: middle bamboo? No \u2014 T2 is Bamboo-2. Not terminal. Not part of any visible meld. T1, T7, T8, T9 discarded \u2014 T2 is early sequence, but no sign of T2-T3-T4 collection. Safe.\n\nB5: Dot-5. Middle tile. No B4,B5,B6 seen. Across has B1,B6,B7,B8 \u2014 B6 is used, but B5 not. Possible they want B5? Unlikely. B5 is generally safe.\n\nW9: Character-9. Terminal. I have one. But W7 was discarded by across player, W8 not seen \u2014 possible W7-W8-W9 was not their goal. Still, terminals are riskier.\n\nW1: I have two. Pair potential. W1 is terminal. Could be useful for Outside Hand, All Terminals, etc. Better to keep.\n\nF4: I have a pair. Good for scoring. Keep.\n\nF2: Seat and Prevailing Wind \u2014 highly valuable. Must keep.\n\nF1: East Wind. No special status. Only one. Can discard.\n\nBut F4 pair is better.\n\nSo candidates: Play T2, Play B5, Play W9, Play F1.\n\nAmong these, T2 and B5 are suited middles \u2014 safer than terminals.\n\nW9 is terminal \u2014 slightly riskier.\n\nF1 is honor \u2014 always somewhat dangerous, but F1 has 2 unshown, and some discarded \u2014 next player played F1, so perhaps not needed.\n\nBut wind honors are often kept.\n\nBest choice: **Play T2**\n\nWhy?\n- T2 is not a wind/dragon.\n- Not a terminal.\n- Not part of any known sequence.\n- Bamboo suit \u2014 across player is playing Dots and Characters, previous is playing Bamboos (T7-T8-T9), but T2 is far from 7-8-9.\n- No indication anyone is collecting T2-T3-T4.\n- Safest among options.\n\nAlternative: Play B5 \u2014 also safe.\n\nBut B5: Dots-5. Across player has B1,B6,B7,B8 \u2014 they might be aiming for Full Flush in B? They have B1 (pung), B6,B7,B8 (chow) \u2014 that\u2019s 6 tiles. If they draw or claim to complete more B, possible. So B5 could be useful to them for B4-B5-B6 or similar.\n\nB5 is adjacent to B6 \u2014 so potentially dangerous.\n\nWhereas T2 is isolated.\n\nAlso, I have only one T2 \u2014 no pair loss.\n\nSo **Play T2** is best.\n\nReason: T2 is a middle, non-terminal, non-honor tile in a suit not currently being aggressively collected by opponents. It poses minimal risk of feeding a winning hand compared to B7 (extremely dangerous), W9 (terminal), or F1/F2 (honors, especially F2 which is Seat/Prevailing Wind). Keeping the F4 pair and potential W1 pair supports future scoring, while discarding T2 maintains safety and flexibility.\n\nAnswer: Play T2"}
